        "name": "tree-model",
        "change": "i-fvvgtgyv",
        "summary": "The stack learns to be an outline",
        "body": "stackTree replaces the rail layout: the stack stays a linearization (landing order is the spine), and dependency structure becomes indentation — a change nests under its deepest dependency (git-nearest on ties), diamond arms beyond the tree edge are kept as annotations, and every row carries its 1-based landing position so grouping by structure never loses the order. File-tree guide columns and elbow flags are computed here, pure and tested; malformed forward/self/unknown deps are dropped so the tree cannot cycle.",
        "from": [
          "apps/desktop/src/renderer/stackgraph.test.ts:0#7ec1",
          "apps/desktop/src/renderer/stackgraph.ts:0#2f6e",
          "apps/desktop/src/renderer/stacktree.test.ts:1#034d",
          "apps/desktop/src/renderer/stacktree.ts:1#ae58"
        ]
      },
      {
        "name": "tree-view",
        "change": "i-nrqx3qcz",
        "summary": "Graph mode grows rings, not wires",
        "body": "The rail rendering answered no user question, so tree mode now draws the outline the stacked-PR world settled on: full-width cards indented under what they build on, guide lines and elbows like a file tree, a #n landing-position badge per row, clickable \"+ needs\" chips for diamond arms (labels truncate, tooltips keep the evidence), and lineage coloring that only adds — upstream accent, downstream graph-5, nothing dimmed into looking disabled. The toggle reads List | Tree; the legacy graph preference maps to tree.",
